course: dictionary
lesson: 3987
title: Brave, polite or grand: the word gallant
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Gallant is a good example. Look at the words around it before you decide.
explain: «Toad, the gallant Water Rat, the famous Mr.» Here gallant comes before a hero's name, so it praises him for being brave and noble. Ask yourself: what kind of person is being described, and what are they doing?
explain: The friends make a gallant attack to win back Toad Hall from the weasels. «A gallant effort» means a brave try, even if it does not succeed. Swap in brave: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Gallant means brave, especially in a fine, noble way: a gallant knight.
fact: A gallant man can also be very polite and kind to women. This is an old-fashioned meaning.
fact: Old books use gallant to mean grand and splendid, as in a gallant ship.
fact: In sports reports, «a gallant effort» means a brave try, even if the team lost.
fact: As a noun, a gallant was an old word for a fashionable young man. It is rarely used today.
exercise: «The gallant knight rode out to save the village.» What does gallant mean here? | Lost in the forest | Very small | *Brave and noble | Lazy and tired
exercise: «The team lost, but it was a gallant effort.» What does gallant suggest? | *A brave try, even though they did not win | A rude and careless try | A try that nobody noticed | A try made in the dark
exercise: Find and copy the word that means brave: «The gallant friends made an attack on the weasels.» | weasels | friends | attack | *gallant
test: «Toad was a gallant hero.» What does gallant most nearly mean? | Rich and greedy | Loud | Sleepy | *Brave and noble
test: Old book: «The gallant ship sailed into the harbour.» What does gallant mean here? | *Grand and splendid to look at | Dirty and broken | Very hungry | Wet and grey
exam: «He was a gallant gentleman who held open every door for the ladies.» Which meaning fits best? | *Very polite and kind to others | Wet and grey | Hungry and tired | Quiet and shy
